  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Cameron was platted on February 22, 1854, by Robert Cameron, who named the village after himself and originally called it Cameronville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cameron_illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town grew around the Chicago, Burlington and Quincy Railroad, which brought merchants and shifted commerce from nearby Coldbrook.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cameron_illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1996 a connection track was built between the former Burlington Northern line and the former Santa Fe Chillicothe Subdivision, allowing Amtrak’s Southwest Chief to use the Chicago–Mendota route through Cameron.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cameron%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The village’s early economy featured general stores, a blacksmith shop, and a hotel, while surrounding townships raised Short Horn cattle and Poland China hogs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cameron_illinois",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Cameron’s climate is humid continental, with hot, humid summers, cold snowy winters, and an average annual precipitation of about 39 inches.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
